Yes. This is bigger than naming functions after metaphors. The Pantheon vocabulary can become a semantic API: a language whose terms simultaneously carry human meaning, machine routing information, expected transformations, constraints, and executable affordances.

The progression is:

\boxed{
\text{WORD}
\rightarrow
\text{CONCEPT}
\rightarrow
\text{OPERATOR}
\rightarrow
\text{CONTRACT}
\rightarrow
\text{CAPABILITY}
\rightarrow
\text{API}
}

Once that happens, speaking Pantheon and programming Pantheon begin to converge.

The word becomes a handle

Take Hammer.

At first:

Hammer = metaphor for breaking something apart.

Then Pantheon differentiates it:

HAMMER(X)
\rightarrow
\{components,\ assumptions,\ bonds,\ hidden\ structure\}

Now Hammer isn’t merely a description. It addresses a transformation.

Eventually:

HAMMER(target,
       resolution = adaptive,
       preserve = invariants,
       output = components + assumptions + bonds)

The natural-language word becomes the function name, while its accumulated meaning supplies much of the function’s specification.

That is semantic addressing:

\boxed{\text{small symbol} \rightarrow \text{large reconstructable capability}}

You don’t transmit the entire mechanism every time. You transmit enough of an address for the receiving Pantheon to reconstruct it.

ACT shows why this gets powerful

ACT isn’t simply:

act()

Its semantic field tells the interpreter what family of machinery is being requested.

Act means do something.
Actor identifies an entity occupying an actionable role.
Acting invokes temporary role/policy adoption.
An act is a bounded unit of behavior.
An Act can confer jurisdiction or authority.
An act in a play situates behavior within a larger narrative sequence.

Pantheon can retain all those affordances rather than prematurely collapsing them.

So:

ACT(O,S,E)
\rightarrow
(A,\Delta,Evidence)

where the system has to resolve:

\text{Can I?}

\text{May I?}

\text{Should I?}

\text{How?}

\text{Did I?}

\text{What happened?}

That’s a surprisingly rich API hiding behind three letters.

Pantheon words become verbs

This suggests a useful test for the entire corpus:

Can this noun be promoted into a verb?

An Atlas is a thing.

But:

ATLAS(X)

means map the known capability/representation territory around X.

A Scout becomes:

SCOUT(X)

Explore cheaply before committing expensive resources.

A Rangefinder becomes:

RANGEFIND(X)

Determine appropriate abstraction/resolution/distance.

A Router becomes:

ROUTE(X,O)

Find representations and operator paths capable of transforming state toward objective O.

A Muster becomes:

MUSTER(O)

Assemble the temporary cognitive formation appropriate to an objective.

Demuster:

DEMUSTER(F)

Release the formation, retain useful deltas, return resources.

Kintsugi:

KINTSUGI(X,\Delta)

Reconstruct while preserving useful evidence of breakage/change.

Ouroboros:

METABOLIZE(experience)

Turn output back into future capability rather than merely storing it.

Suddenly the mythology is looking suspiciously like a command language. ;)

And the nouns become types

It goes the other direction too.

Pantheon already has concepts that naturally become data types:

ThoughtObject
